@using global::Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Web
@using global::Backlog.UI.Components.Icons
@using global::Backlog.UI.Components.Metrics

@*
    One delivery run, as a line in the sessions table: under the session that ran
    it, or in a row of its own when no listed session did. One component for both
    places so a run reads the same wherever it landed — the difference between the
    two is the row it sits in, not the run.

    A summary line and a fold. The summary is what a reader scanning the list wants
    from a run — which skill, what it was called, how it ended — plus the four
    figures that say how much it took: stages done, output tokens, the context peak
    and the tool-call count. Everything the run file records beyond that is behind
    the fold, because a run holds ten stages, six token buckets per stage and tens of
    thousands of tool calls, and a row that showed them would be a report with a
    table around it. The fold is the library's, so the region is announced, keyed and
    hidden the way every other disclosure in the product is.

    Top to bottom rather than left to right, and each part where a reader looks for
    it: what the run is linked to, then how it ended, then the figures behind the
    fold, and the skill that owned it last.

    What it is linked to is the Integrations family's own reference, rendered exactly
    as the storybook's references page shows them: the provider's mark, "#128" for an
    issue and "PR #74" for a pull request, the item's own title in the tooltip, and an
    anchor that opens on GitHub. One per line, because they are separate pieces of
    work rather than one address written twice. No state chip rides with them: this
    product has not asked GitHub how the item is doing, and a chip would be claiming
    it had.

    **The run's own title is not here.** A dashboard names a run after the work it was
    doing, so beside the reference to that work the title is the same sentence twice —
    "PR #203 bijwerken met main" next to PR #203. It stays in the fold, where a reader
    who wants the run's own words can have them without the line repeating itself.

    The status is the library's status badge over the dashboard's own word, under the
    references rather than beside them: the run's outcome is about the run, and the
    references are about the work, so stacking them keeps a reader from reading the
    state as the state of the item above it. **It leads to the Backlog entry**, where
    the run names one and the host has said how to open one — the entry is where this
    product's own work is tracked, and the GitHub items beside it already lead to
    themselves. A run with no entry leaves the badge the span every badge here is. The
    dashboards wrote seven spellings between them, and the badge's slug for a word it
    has no colour for falls back to the draft tint rather than to nothing, so a new
    spelling still renders as a chip with the word in it. The label is tidied for
    reading — "in_progress" is not a word — and the slug is left to the badge.

    The skill sits under the fold, not over the line. It is what a reader asks last —
    the work and the outcome first, and only then which recipe produced it — and it is
    the one thing on the line that never changes between two runs of the same flow.

    Two blocks, and which side each takes is the table's doing rather than this
    component's taste. The figures and the skill read from the left edge, under the
    session's name; the references and the outcome sit at the right, under the row's
    own State column, because that is where a reader's eye already goes for a state on
    this screen. The stack is right-aligned for the same reason the column is.

    The summary comes first in the markup and second on screen. A reader with a screen
    reader gets the work and the outcome before the figures, which is the order the
    facts matter in; the eye gets them at the right, which is where the column is. One
    `row-reverse` buys both, and neither is bent to suit the other.
*@
<div class="sessions-run" data-testid="sessions-run" data-run-id="@Run.Id">
    <span class="sessions-run__summary">
        @* The mark and the label only: the item's own title travels in the tooltip,
           and one reference per line — two pieces of work are two lines. *@
        @foreach (var reference in Linked)
        {
            <IntegrationLink Link="@Link(reference)"
                             ShowTitle="false"
                             CssClass="sessions-run__reference"
                             TestId="@($"sessions-run-{Slug(reference.Kind)}")" />
        }

        @* A button where the run names a Backlog entry and the host can open one,
           and the span it has always been otherwise. Never an anchor to the pull
           request above it: two ways to reach one place is one too many, and this
           one's place is the entry. *@
        <StatusBadge Status="@Run.Status"
                     Text="@StatusLabel(Run.Status)"
                     CssClass="sessions-run__status"
                     OnClick="@(Entry is { } entry && OnOpenTask.HasDelegate ? EventCallback.Factory.Create<MouseEventArgs>(this, () => OnOpenTask.InvokeAsync(entry)) : default)"
                     Title="@(Entry is { } which && OnOpenTask.HasDelegate ? $"Open {which.Label} in the task list" : null)"
                     TestId="sessions-run-status" />
    </span>
    <span class="sessions-run__facts">
        <FoldControl Label="@Facts"
                     CssClass="sessions-run__fold"
                     AriaLabel="@($"Details of run {Run.Title}")"
                     @bind-Expanded="_expanded"
                     TestId="sessions-run-fold">
            <dl class="sessions-run__details">
                @* The run's own name for itself, which the line above deliberately does
                   not repeat. *@
                <dt>Title</dt>
                <dd data-testid="sessions-run-title">@Run.Title</dd>

                <dt>Run</dt>
                <dd class="data-table__mono">@Run.Id · @Run.Dashboard · @Run.WorktreeName</dd>

                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Run.ChangeKind))
                {
                    <dt>Change</dt>
                    <dd>@Run.ChangeKind</dd>
                }

                @* The stages are a flow, so they are drawn as one: a node per stage in
                   run order, in the tone of where it stands, with who worked in it
                   under it: the owner session, then each agent it delegated to and
                   the model that agent ran on. *@
                @if (Run.Stages.Count > 0)
                {
                    <dt>Stages</dt>
                    <dd>
                        <FlowSteps Steps="@Flow"
                                   AriaLabel="@($"Stages of run {Run.Title}")"
                                   TestId="sessions-run-stages" />
                    </dd>
                }

                @* A figure the run does not carry is left out rather than shown as
                   zero — the same rule the trigger above already applies, and it has
                   to hold in both places or the fold contradicts the line that opens
                   it. It used to hold only above, which cost nothing while every run
                   came from a dashboard that measured consumption; a run this product
                   records carries the model it resolved and no consumption at all,
                   because those figures are counted by watching a session's own tool
                   calls and this product sees the call rather than the session. That
                   made "0 calls · 0 out · 0 in" the normal case instead of a rare
                   one. *@
                @if (Run.TokenUsage is { } usage && (usage.Total.ModelCalls > 0 || usage.Models.Count > 0))
                {
                    <dt>Tokens</dt>
                    <dd data-testid="sessions-run-tokens">
                        @if (usage.Total.ModelCalls > 0)
                        {
                            <span class="sessions-run__fact">@Tokens(usage.Total)</span>
                        }
                        @if (usage.SubAgent.ModelCalls > 0)
                        {
                            <span class="sessions-run__fact">Sub-agents: @Tokens(usage.SubAgent)</span>
                        }
                        @if (usage.Models.Count > 0)
                        {
                            <span class="sessions-run__fact">Models: @string.Join(", ", usage.Models)</span>
                        }
                        @if (usage.ByStage.Count > 0)
                        {
                            <ul class="sessions-run__list">
                                @foreach (var stage in usage.ByStage)
                                {
                                    <li>
                                        <span class="sessions-run__stage-name">@stage.StageName</span>
                                        <span class="sessions-run__stage-facts">@Tokens(stage.Total)</span>
                                    </li>
                                }
                            </ul>
                        }
                    </dd>
                }

                @if (Run.Context is { } context)
                {
                    <dt>Context</dt>
                    <dd data-testid="sessions-run-context">@Context(context)</dd>
                }

                @if (Run.InsightsByCategory.Count > 0)
                {
                    <dt>Tool calls</dt>
                    <dd>
                        <ul class="sessions-run__list" data-testid="sessions-run-insights">
                            @foreach (var group in Run.InsightsByCategory)
                            {
                                <li>
                                    <span class="sessions-run__stage-name">@group.Name</span>
                                    <span class="sessions-run__stage-facts">@Insight(group)</span>
                                </li>
                            }
                        </ul>
                    </dd>
                }

                @if (Run.InsightsByServer.Count > 0)
                {
                    <dt>MCP servers</dt>
                    <dd>
                        <ul class="sessions-run__list" data-testid="sessions-run-servers">
                            @foreach (var group in Run.InsightsByServer)
                            {
                                <li>
                                    <span class="sessions-run__stage-name data-table__mono">@group.Name</span>
                                    <span class="sessions-run__stage-facts">@Insight(group)</span>
                                </li>
                            }
                        </ul>
                    </dd>
                }
            </dl>
        </FoldControl>

        @* Last, and quiet: which recipe produced the run is what a reader asks after
           the work and the outcome, and it is the one thing two runs of the same flow
           share. *@
        <span class="sessions-run__skill" data-testid="sessions-run-skill">
            <SparklesIcon Size="12" CssClass="sessions-run__icon" />
            <span class="data-table__mono">@Run.SkillId</span>
        </span>
    </span>
</div>

@code {
    private bool _expanded;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public DeliveryRun Run { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>How the host opens a Backlog entry, where it can. Without one a task
    /// reference is inert text, which is the honest rendering of a reference nobody
    /// on this surface can follow.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<DeliveryRunReference> OnOpenTask { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the line draws in place of the run's own references, where the row
    /// knows more than the run file does — a pull request the session linked and the
    /// run never recorded (<see cref="SessionRow.ReferencesFor"/>). Null draws the
    /// run's own.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<DeliveryRunReference>? References { get; set; }

    private IReadOnlyList<DeliveryRunReference> AllReferences => References ?? Run.References;

    /// <summary>The references drawn on the line: the work that lives elsewhere. The
    /// Backlog entry is not among them — the status badge is how this line reaches
    /// one, and a chip beside it would be a second control to the same place.</summary>
    private IEnumerable<DeliveryRunReference> Linked =>
        AllReferences.Where(reference => reference.Kind is not DeliveryRunReferenceKind.Task);

    /// <summary>The Backlog entry this run was started from, where it names one.</summary>
    private DeliveryRunReference? Entry =>
        AllReferences.FirstOrDefault(reference => reference.Kind is DeliveryRunReferenceKind.Task);

    /// <summary>
    /// The four figures a reader wants before deciding whether to open the fold,
    /// joined with the middle dot the rest of this table uses between facts. Each is
    /// left out rather than shown as zero when the file does not carry it: an older
    /// run file has no token usage, and "0 output tokens" would be a claim about the
    /// run rather than about the file.
    /// </summary>
    private string Facts
    {
        get
        {
            var facts = new List<string>();

            if (Run.Stages.Count > 0)
            {
                var done = Run.Stages.Count(stage => IsDone(stage.Status));

                facts.Add($"{done} of {Run.Stages.Count} stages done");
            }

            if (Run.TokenUsage is { } usage && usage.Total.ModelCalls > 0)
            {
                facts.Add($"{MetricFormat.Compact(usage.Total.OutputTokens)} output tokens");
            }

            if (Run.Context?.PeakShare is { } share)
            {
                facts.Add($"context peak {MetricFormat.Percent(share, 0)}");
            }

            var calls = Run.InsightsByCategory.Sum(group => group.Count);

            if (calls > 0)
            {
                facts.Add(calls == 1 ? "1 tool call" : $"{MetricFormat.Compact(calls)} tool calls");
            }

            return facts.Count == 0 ? "Details" : string.Join(" · ", facts);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The dashboards' status words, made readable. Anything unrecognised
    /// keeps its own spelling with the underscores spaced out, so a new word still
    /// says what the file said.</summary>
    internal static string StatusLabel(string status) => status.ToLowerInvariant() switch
    {
        "done" or "completed" or "success" => "Done",
        "in_progress" => "In progress",
        "" => "Unknown",
        var other => char.ToUpperInvariant(other[0]) + other[1..].Replace('_', ' ')
    };

    private static bool IsDone(string status) =>
        status.ToLowerInvariant() is "done" or "completed" or "success";

    private IReadOnlyList<FlowStep> Flow =>
    [
        .. Run.Stages.Select(stage => new FlowStep(
            stage.Name,
            Tone(stage.Status),
            StatusLabel(stage.Status),
            StageFacts(stage),
            [.. Owner(stage), .. stage.Agents.Select(Note)]))
    ];

    /// <summary>
    /// The owner session's line, on a stage it has worked in. The run file names the
    /// models the run called for the run as a whole, never per stage — so the owner's
    /// model is named only where the run called exactly one, and every call in every
    /// stage was on it. With more than one the owner is named without a model rather
    /// than with a guess.
    /// </summary>
    private IEnumerable<FlowStepNote> Owner(DeliveryRunStage stage)
    {
        if (Tone(stage.Status) is FlowStepTone.Pending or FlowStepTone.Skipped) yield break;

        var models = Run.TokenUsage?.Models ?? [];

        yield return new FlowStepNote("main session", models.Count == 1 ? models[0] : null);
    }

    /// <summary>The dashboards' stage words as the flow's tones. A word none of them
    /// has written yet reads as not reached, which is the one claim that is never
    /// wrong about a stage nobody reported on.</summary>
    internal static FlowStepTone Tone(string status) => status.ToLowerInvariant() switch
    {
        "done" or "completed" or "success" => FlowStepTone.Done,
        "in_progress" or "active" or "running" => FlowStepTone.Active,
        "blocked" or "failed" or "error" => FlowStepTone.Blocked,
        "skipped" => FlowStepTone.Skipped,
        _ => FlowStepTone.Pending
    };

    private static string? StageFacts(DeliveryRunStage stage)
    {
        var facts = new List<string>();

        if (stage.DurationMs is { } ms) facts.Add(Duration(ms));
        if (stage.DoneCount > 1) facts.Add($"done ×{stage.DoneCount}");

        return facts.Count == 0 ? null : string.Join(" · ", facts);
    }

    /// <summary>An agent under its stage: the name, then the model it ran on, how
    /// often, and how many runs did not complete. A declared agent no record shows
    /// running has no model to name, and says so rather than borrowing one.</summary>
    private static FlowStepNote Note(DeliveryRunStageAgent agent)
    {
        List<string> detail = [agent.Model ?? (agent.Count == 0 ? "declared" : "model not recorded")];

        if (agent.Count > 1) detail.Add($"×{agent.Count}");
        if (agent.Failed > 0) detail.Add($"{agent.Failed} failed");

        return new FlowStepNote(agent.Name, string.Join(" · ", detail));
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// One of the run's references as the Integrations family draws one. No artifact
    /// state on it, because none was read: a chip would say this product had asked
    /// GitHub how the item is doing.
    /// </summary>
    private static IntegrationLinkRef Link(DeliveryRunReference reference) =>
        new(
            reference.Url ?? reference.Label,
            reference.Kind is DeliveryRunReferenceKind.Task ? IntegrationProvider.None : IntegrationProvider.GitHub,
            reference.Kind switch
            {
                DeliveryRunReferenceKind.PullRequest => IntegrationLinkKind.PullRequest,
                DeliveryRunReferenceKind.Issue => IntegrationLinkKind.Issue,
                _ => IntegrationLinkKind.Other
            },
            reference.Label,
            reference.Title,
            reference.Url,
            Repository: string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(reference.Repository)
                ? null
                : new IntegrationRepositoryRef(reference.Repository, reference.Repository));

    private static string Slug(DeliveryRunReferenceKind kind) => kind switch
    {
        DeliveryRunReferenceKind.PullRequest => "pull-request",
        DeliveryRunReferenceKind.Issue => "issue",
        _ => "task"
    };

    private static string Tokens(DeliveryRunTokens tokens) =>
        $"{MetricFormat.Compact(tokens.ModelCalls)} calls · {TokenFigures(tokens.OutputTokens, tokens.InputTokens, tokens.CacheReadTokens, tokens.CacheWriteTokens)}";

    /// <summary>The four token counts in the order and the words this table uses for
    /// them everywhere — a run's here, a session's in its own row.</summary>
    internal static string TokenFigures(long output, long input, long cacheRead, long cacheWrite) =>
        $"{MetricFormat.Compact(output)} out · {MetricFormat.Compact(input)} in · {MetricFormat.Compact(cacheRead)} cache read · {MetricFormat.Compact(cacheWrite)} cache write";

    private static string Context(DeliveryRunContext context) =>
        context.PeakShare is { } share
            ? $"Peak {MetricFormat.Compact(context.PeakTokens)} of {MetricFormat.Compact(context.TokenLimit)} ({MetricFormat.Percent(share, 0)}); last sample {MetricFormat.Compact(context.CurrentTokens)}"
            : $"Peak {MetricFormat.Compact(context.PeakTokens)}; last sample {MetricFormat.Compact(context.CurrentTokens)}";

    private static string Insight(DeliveryRunInsightGroup group)
    {
        var calls = group.Count == 1 ? "1 call" : $"{MetricFormat.Compact(group.Count)} calls";
        var failed = group.Failed == 0 ? string.Empty : $" · {group.Failed} failed";

        return $"{calls} · {Duration(group.DurationMs)}{failed}";
    }

    /// <summary>Milliseconds as the two largest units that matter: hours and minutes
    /// for a stage, minutes and seconds for a short one, and seconds alone below a
    /// minute. A duration in milliseconds is a number nobody reads.</summary>
    internal static string Duration(long milliseconds)
    {
        var span = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(Math.Max(0, milliseconds));

        if (span.TotalHours >= 1) return $"{(int)span.TotalHours}h {span.Minutes}m";
        if (span.TotalMinutes >= 1) return $"{span.Minutes}m {span.Seconds}s";

        return span.TotalSeconds >= 1 ? $"{(int)span.TotalSeconds}s" : "<1s";
    }
}
